# Copy this file to .env and add your key:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored. Never commit a real key.
#
# This tool calls an LLM once per message — that is what it is for — so a key is REQUIRED.
# There is no offline mode: with no key set, `pnpm dev` refuses to start and tells you this.


# ── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An OpenRouter key. Create one at https://openrouter.ai/keys — it takes about two minutes, and
# triaging the whole 13-message sample costs a fraction of a cent.
#
# The key is read by the Vite dev server and stays in the Node process. It is never sent to the
# browser, never bundled, and never written to disk by this app. Note the absence of a VITE_ prefix:
# a VITE_ variable is compiled into client code where anyone can read it, so the only thing that may
# ever go in one is an address — never a credential.
#
# Set a spend cap on the key. Any key without a ceiling is a bad idea, however careful you are.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=


# ── Optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which model triages. Defaults to a cheap, structured-output-capable model; see prompts/README.md
# for how this was chosen and what the alternatives measured.
# TRIAGE_MODEL=meta-llama/llama-3.1-8b-instruct

# How many messages are triaged concurrently. Default 4. Raise it and you will meet the provider's
# rate limit; lower it and a 13-message queue just takes longer.
# TRIAGE_CONCURRENCY=4

# Where the SQLite file lives. Default ./data/triage.db, created on first run.
# TRIAGE_DB=./data/triage.db
